 ```bash
 python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_bge_replay_cli.py -v
 ```
+
+### Fixed-case and random-case performance scenarios
+
+Use `--case-id case-0002` to prepare an actual one-case request pool. The five
+source samples are still validated; only the selected sample is packed into the
+new plan. Omit `--case-id` for uniform random replay with replacement of all five
+samples. Unknown IDs are rejected before creating the output directory.
+
+```bash
+python3 models/bge/replay.py --prepare-only \
+  --config models/bge/config.yaml --device 10 \
+  --output-dir work/bge/replay/fixed-case-0002-new \
+  --case-id case-0002 --instances 1 --seed 2027 \
+  --min-seconds 60 --max-seconds 300 --min-requests 10000
+
+python3 models/bge/replay.py --prepare-only \
+  --config models/bge/config.yaml --device 10 \
+  --output-dir work/bge/replay/random-five-new \
+  --instances 1 --seed 2027 \
+  --min-seconds 60 --max-seconds 300 --min-requests 10000
+```
+
+The plan records `workload.kind`, the actual `case_ids`, and the sampling rule.
+The existing native replay sampler repeats the only entry for a one-case pool;
+changing the seed does not change that fixed input. All requests retain complete
+H2D, execution synchronization and D2H. These commands only prepare files; submit
+plans through the managed launcher after checking device availability.
+
+Build a separate baseline for each scenario and compare like with like. Final
+reports show both scenarios using the same frozen delivery configuration, with
+separate QPS and p99 values and adoption decisions. A per-case subset extracted
+from a random run is not a fixed-case benchmark. All existing 2026-10-26 results
+are random-five measurements; fixed-case performance has not yet been measured.
+Full five-case correctness, request-output pressure and mixed-shape stability
+remain required. Additional runs consume the existing device-time budget.
